{"kind":"artifact","text":"The onboarding-identity core at exact head 33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86 on request/onboarding-identity-core, pushed to origin, one repair commit on 358a46d4 which sits directly on main c19f4eb9, so it fast-forwards. This is the implementation report for request 2c9bf640 under promise 2999b16a. It replaces the withdrawn artifact 68bd30a5 and answers both defects codex found in the first wave.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. host/identity, in the host layer, so an application inherits identity rather than reinventing it and a host reads it without holding an application profile. Three fixed schema families: a witness declaration, an anchor, and a withdrawal. An anchor says one signing key belongs to a persistent identity, for this repository, within a scope, until an expiry. Vouching is never claimed in a payload, only derived from which key signed the record. A witness declaration has force only when the key that initialized the repository signed it, and cannot mint outside its declared schemes. Any other endorsement is a delegation which names no identity and inherits its endorser's. Expiry and withdrawal are judged against the sequencer's signed timestamp on the record being folded, never a reader's clock. The GitHub rung runs outside the fold; only its result is signed in.\n\nREPAIR ONE, SCOPE. The exported SelfSigned vouching value could not be produced. Witnessing was the only ground assignment in resolve.go, and the delegation branch's min(SelfSigned, parent.vouching) had no base case of its own, so no log could resolve a key to it. The extracted scope names only GitHub. The constant and its String case are removed; a delegation now inherits parent.vouching outright, which is what the code already computed. The package doc and docs/reference/architecture.md now say one vouching rung is implemented — witnessed — and state the self-signed rung as deferred along with the providers that would occupy it, Nostr and a published forge signing key. The axis stays ordered so that adding it later is one more value and the reduction rule the verification axis already follows.\n\nREPAIR TWO, THE TOKEN. GitHub.Check promised the bearer token never reaches an error and had two seams where it did. It returned response.Status verbatim, and HTTP/1.1 carries a provider's own reason phrase unaltered, so a provider echoing what it was sent put the credential into an error. It also wrapped the client.Do error, and the client is the deployment's to set, so a round-tripper reporting the request it was handed published the Authorization header. Both are closed by one rule with no exceptions: no byte of provider- or transport-controlled text reaches an error from here. A refusal reports the numeric code with Go's own reason phrase, generated locally by statusText from the number. A transport failure returns a fixed local error, and a JSON decode failure returns a fixed unreadable-answer error with no provider-derived detail. The caller's own cancellation still surfaces, because ctx.Err() is the caller's answer and not the transport's. There is no substring redaction anywhere in the package and no withoutToken helper: redaction removes only the spelling it goes looking for, and the party echoing the credential chooses the spelling.\n\nTESTS ACTUALLY RUN, at this exact head. Two adversarial tests carry the security repair. TestGitHubCheckReportsNothingFromTheProvidersStatusLine uses a raw-socket server that writes the token into the reason phrase, which httptest cannot do because its server composes the phrase from Go's own table; it asserts the token is absent from the error and that the canonical 401 Unauthorized this program built is present. TestGitHubCheckReportsNothingFromATransportError installs a transport whose error echoes the Authorization header, and asserts neither the token nor the transport's own words appear. TestGitHubCheckStillReportsTheCallersOwnCancellation holds the one distinction kept.\n\nFAIL-FIRST PROOF. Before committing, both guards were reverted to the original returns — response.Status, and the wrapped client.Do error — and the three tests were run against that unrepaired behaviour. All three failed, each printing the literal token: \"401 Unauthorized-gho_reason_phrase_secret\" from the reason phrase, and \"dial refused while sending Authorization: Bearer gho_transport_secret\" from the transport, with the cancellation test reporting the transport error where it wanted context.Canceled. The guards were then restored and the same tests pass. The assertions are written from the desired behaviour, not transcribed from output.\n\nGATES, all at 33843691. gofmt -l over the Go tree: clean. go build ./...: ok. go vet ./...: ok. go test -count=1 ./...: every package ok, including internal/docset, which is the citation gate over the architecture edit, and host/identity. go test -race -count=1 ./...: every package ok, no data race reported.\n\nCONDITIONS. The public surface is host/identity's exported API and nothing else; it imports host and no application profile, gates no append, and reads no clock. Nothing outside host/identity and docs/reference/architecture.md changed in this repair. The exported surface loses one constant and no behaviour any log could reach.\n\nWHAT IS DEFERRED AND SAID SO. The self-signed vouching rung and its providers. Authenticated shared-host support: witness custody belongs to the deployment under the supported single-operator posture. The two-axis display surface belongs to the application. This package authenticates nobody and authorizes nothing.","body":{"commit":"33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86","path":"host"}}